<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jagatjit Singh</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1872">1872</a>–<a href="/years/1949">1949</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Jagatjit Singh of Kapurthala</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jagatjit Singh was maharaja of Kapurthala, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> princely state in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, from 1877 to 1947. A widely travelled Francophile, he built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagatjit-palace">Jagatjit Palace</a> modelled on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a> and became known for his lavish court and European marriages. He represented <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> in the 1920s. His Sikh dynasty ruled a state with a Muslim majority, and Kapurthala acceded to India in 1947.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jagatjit-singh.jpg" alt="Jagatjit Singh" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bourne and Shepherd · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Photograph_of_Maharaja_Jagatjit_Singh_of_Kapurthala_State,_by_Bourne_and_Shepherd,_ca.1900%27s%E2%80%931910%27s_(detail).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
